# JWT authentication

Runtime: PyJWT + pwdlib (`uv add pyjwt "pwdlib[argon2]"`). Default is symmetric HS256; add
`pyjwt[crypto]` only when another service must verify tokens with RS256/ES256 public keys.
FastAPI APIs only; static operator token gates stay in `python-fastapi`. Install with the
database bundle: authentication needs the `users` ORM model and repository.

## Layout

| Module | Layer | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| `project/libs/security.py` | libs | Pure helpers — hash/verify passwords, create/decode tokens — and the `TokenExpiredError` / `TokenInvalidError` exceptions they raise (render [`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md) here, copy only if missing) |
| `project/components/users/models.py` | repositories | `User` ORM model (below) |
| `project/components/users/repositories.py` | repositories | `UserRepository` (below) |
| `project/components/users/schemas.py` | libs | `Token` / `CurrentUser` schemas |
| `project/components/users/endpoints.py` | presentation | Login endpoint, `get_current_user` / `get_current_active_user` |

`security.py` imports no FastAPI and no ORM — every component may use it. Merge into
`SettingsValidator` (`python-architecture/python-settings.mdc`): `SECRET_KEY: SecretStr`
(required, no default — generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`; reuse the sqladmin panel's
`SECRET_KEY` when it is installed), `JWT_ALGORITHM: str = "HS256"`,
`ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES: int = 30`.

Helpers contract:

- `create_access_token(subject, expires_delta)` encodes `sub` (string) and `exp` (UTC). Keep
  `sub` a unique stable string per subject app-wide — `str(user.id)`; prefix it (`"user:1"`)
  when several subject kinds exist. PyJWT checks `exp` on decode.
- `verify_password` returns `(valid, new_hash)`: new hashes are Argon2, stored bcrypt hashes
  still verify and return `new_hash` — persist the rehash on login instead of migrating
  passwords out of band. Verify against `DUMMY_HASH` when the user is missing so response
  timing does not reveal valid usernames.
- `decode_token(token)` returns the `sub` string and raises `TokenExpiredError` when `exp`
  has passed or `TokenInvalidError` for any other invalid or tampered token — both `AuthError`
  subclasses living in `security.py` (`python-architecture/python-exceptions.mdc`), so callers
  import no third-party types and PyJWT's expired-vs-invalid distinction survives the wrap.

## User model

`project/components/users/models.py` follows `python-sqlalchemy` (`Base` / `TimeMixin`,
BigInteger primary keys, domain id types). Authentication requires these fields:

```python
from sqlalchemy import BigInteger, Boolean, String
from sqlalchemy.orm import Mapped, mapped_column

from project.base.models import Base, TimeMixin
from project.datatypes import UserIdT


class User(Base, TimeMixin):
    __tablename__ = "users"

    id: Mapped[UserIdT] = mapped_column(BigInteger, primary_key=True, autoincrement=True)
    email: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(320), unique=True, index=True)
    password_hash: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(255))
    is_active: Mapped[bool] = mapped_column(Boolean, default=True)
```

`BigInteger` is passed explicitly: `Mapped[UserIdT]` resolves no Core type on its own — a
NewType over `int` degrades to `Integer`, and over `Annotated` fails at mapping. Registering
your id types once in `Base.type_annotation_map` (`{UserIdT: BigInteger}`) is the
once-per-repo alternative (`python-sqlalchemy`).

| Field | Contract | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | BigInteger PK, domain type `UserIdT` | token `sub`, user loading |
| `email` | unique + indexed, `String(320)`; substitute `username` with the same constraints when the repo logs in by name | login lookup |
| `password_hash` | `String(255)` (Argon2 ≈ 97 chars, bcrypt 60); never in admin forms (`python-sqladmin`), never logged | `verify_password` |
| `is_active` | `default=True` | `get_current_active_user` |

When the repo already has a `User` model, require these fields on it instead of adding a
second identity model. Register the model with a `SQLAlchemyFactory` in `tests/factories.py`
(`python-polyfactory`) and generate an Alembic revision after adding fields
(`python-alembic`).

## Repository

`UserRepository` extends the generic base (`BASE_REPOSITORIES.md` →
`project/base/repositories.py`); it needs only the login lookup — `get_or_none`,
`update_fields_and_save`, `new`, and `save` come from `ORMRepository`.

```python
from sqlalchemy import select

from project.base.repositories import ORMRepository
from project.components.users.models import User


class UserRepository(ORMRepository[User]):
    _model = User

    @classmethod
    async def get_by_email(cls, email: str) -> User | None:
        async with cls.get_session() as session:
            return (await session.scalars(select(User).where(User.email == email))).first()
```

Register once in `project/container.py`
(`python-architecture/python-di.mdc`):

```python
user_repository = LazyService("project.components.users.repositories:UserRepository")
```

## Endpoints

Schemas first — `project/components/users/schemas.py`:

```python
from pydantic import BaseModel

from project.datatypes import UserIdT


class Token(BaseModel):
    access_token: str
    token_type: str = "bearer"


class CurrentUser(BaseModel):
    id: UserIdT
    email: str
    is_active: bool
```

Dependencies return the `CurrentUser` schema, never the ORM `User`: presentation imports no
models, the admin panel stays the only presentation-side model importer (`python-sqladmin`).

`tokenUrl` must equal the versioned login path (`python-fastapi` URL versioning). Raise
`HTTPException` only in `endpoints.py`; the app exception handlers shape the body.

```python
from datetime import timedelta
from typing import Annotated

from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, HTTPException
from fastapi.security import OAuth2PasswordBearer, OAuth2PasswordRequestForm

from project.libs.security import (
    DUMMY_HASH,
    TokenExpiredError,
    TokenInvalidError,
    create_access_token,
    decode_token,
    verify_password,
)
from project.components.users.schemas import CurrentUser, Token
from project.container import Container
from project.settings import Settings

router = APIRouter(prefix="/users")
oauth2_scheme = OAuth2PasswordBearer(tokenUrl="users/v1/token")


@router.post("/v1/token")
async def login(form: Annotated[OAuth2PasswordRequestForm, Depends()]) -> Token:
    user = await Container().user_repository.get_by_email(form.username)
    valid, new_hash = verify_password(form.password, user.password_hash if user else DUMMY_HASH)
    if user is None or not valid:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=401,
            detail="Incorrect email or password",
            headers={"WWW-Authenticate": "Bearer"},
        )
    if new_hash:
        await Container().user_repository.update_fields_and_save(user, password_hash=new_hash)
    return Token(
        access_token=create_access_token(
            user.id,
            timedelta(minutes=Settings().ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES),
        )
    )


async def get_current_user(token: Annotated[str, Depends(oauth2_scheme)]) -> CurrentUser:
    try:
        user_id = int(decode_token(token))
    except TokenExpiredError as e:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=401,
            detail="Token has expired",
            headers={"WWW-Authenticate": "Bearer"},
        ) from e
    except (TokenInvalidError, ValueError) as e:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=401,
            detail="Could not validate credentials",
            headers={"WWW-Authenticate": "Bearer"},
        ) from e
    user = await Container().user_repository.get_or_none(user_id)
    if user is None:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=401,
            detail="Could not validate credentials",
            headers={"WWW-Authenticate": "Bearer"},
        )
    return CurrentUser(id=user.id, email=user.email, is_active=user.is_active)


async def get_current_active_user(
    current_user: Annotated[CurrentUser, Depends(get_current_user)],
) -> CurrentUser:
    if not current_user.is_active:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail="Inactive user")
    return current_user
```

Protect routes with the dependency — per route or the whole router. Wrap protected responses
in the shared envelope when the repo uses one (`ApiResponseSchema[T]` from
`project/base/schemas.py`, `python-fastapi`); `Token` stays bare — the OAuth2 password flow
and Swagger UI's Authorize button read `access_token` from the top level, an envelope breaks
the authorize flow:

```python
from project.base.schemas import ApiResponseSchema


@router.get("/v1/me")
async def me(
    current_user: Annotated[CurrentUser, Depends(get_current_active_user)],
) -> ApiResponseSchema[CurrentUser]:
    return ApiResponseSchema(data=current_user)


orders = APIRouter(dependencies=[Depends(get_current_user)])
```

Other components import the dependencies from the users endpoints (presentation may import
presentation). Write paths store `get_password_hash(...)` in `password_hash`; never log
tokens or password hashes.

## layers.toml

```toml
[libs.jwt]
allowed_in = ["libs"]

[libs.pwdlib]
allowed_in = ["libs"]
```

`jwt` and `pwdlib` stay inside `project/libs/security.py`; components import the helpers, not
the libraries.

## Tests

Cover the flow in e2e (`python-tests`) with a factory-built user (`password_hash` =
`get_password_hash(...)`): login rejects a wrong password and an unknown user (401), a valid
token reaches a protected route, tampered tokens are rejected with "Could not validate
credentials" while an expired one answers "Token has expired", `is_active=False` gets 400
through `get_current_active_user`. Expired tokens: create one with
`create_access_token(user.id, timedelta(seconds=-1))` instead of waiting or freezing the
clock. Do not `patch` `jwt` or pwdlib (`patch-linter`).
